g++-14 miscompiles this tree at -O3 -flto -march=native. Root cause not found.
Found 2026-08-23. CLAUDE.md has the summary; this is the reproduction.

  To reproduce, on g++-14 (14.2.0 here) on an AVX2 host:

    1. Check out main. Add one inert member to SystemSolver, in the private
       block next to `steadyDiagnostics`:

           bool reproPaddingFlag = false;
           std::vector<double> reproPadding;

       Nothing reads either. Add `#include <vector>` with the other includes.
    2. `make clean && make test`
    3. Run the one test in a loop:

           for i in $(seq 1 12); do
             Tests/UnitTests/UnitTests --run_test=algebraic_derivative_tests/\
               the_assembled_jacobian_matches_a_finite_difference_of_the_residual
           done

    Without the member: 12/12 pass. With it: 8/12 fail, always the same way --
    the AuxDiffusion cases, plain and superconvergent, at k = 1, 2 and 3, drift
    ~1.24.

    NOTE: `algebraic_derivative_tests` no longer exists -- it went with the dG/dt
    gate, the only thing that read the algebraic time derivatives -- so step 3
    cannot be run as written. The miscompilation was never diagnosed, only
    bounded, so this needs a fresh reproduction rather than deletion. What
    mattered was a dense finite-difference of residual() inlined into a test
    translation unit plus a change to SystemSolver's member layout;
    SolveJacTests.cpp differences the residual the same way and is where to
    start.

  The signature, which is how to recognise it if it turns up elsewhere: the
  differenced Jacobian loses the column of the first interior trace DOF
  (index 33 / 49 / 65 at k = 1 / 2 / 3, always lambda[1]), giving 0 against a
  stable assembled -1.5, so the drift is 1.5 / 1.2071067811865475 =
  1.2426406871192851. Passing runs are bit-identical to each other; failing runs
  all differ from each other.

  Established:

  * Needs all three of g++-14, `-flto` and `-march=native` (AVX2, znver2 here).
    Drop any one -> 10/10 clean. `-O0` clean. g++-15 clean. clang++-19 clean.
    (g++-13 does not build this tree, so it was not tested.)
  * Not build staleness, not leftover output files, not ASLR (`setarch -R` with a
    fixed environment still flakes), not BLAS threading, not uninitialised
    trivial automatics (`-ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero` and `=pattern` both still
    flake). AddressSanitizer: 12/12 clean, no invalid access.
  * The solver binary is *not* observably affected: `MaNTA` on
    Tests/RegressionTests/AuxVarTest.conf gave bit-identical netCDF over 8 runs
    with the member in place. `MaNTA` is built without -DTEST from a different
    object set, which points at the header-only `fdjac::jacobian` inlined into
    the test TU rather than at the solver.

  The obstacle, and the thing to know before spending an afternoon on it: **every
  codegen perturbation hides it.** Adding a read of Y, extending an existing
  BOOST_TEST_MESSAGE, storing into a file-scope array, `-fno-strict-aliasing`,
  `-fno-tree-vectorize` -- all take it to 10/10 clean. So a flag that "fixes" it
  is not evidence about the mechanism; `-fno-strict-aliasing` in particular looks
  like a smoking gun and is not one. The only asymmetric signal is that adding a
  member to SystemSolver reliably *creates* it.

  Next steps, roughly in order of expected value:

  * Diff the g++-14 assembly for the test TU with and without the inert member,
    around the inlined `fdjac::jacobian` loop. This is the only route left that
    does not perturb the thing being measured.
  * If that shows a genuine miscompile, reduce it and report it upstream; g++-14
    is still a supported distro compiler and CI has two required legs on it.
  * If it shows the compiler exploiting UB, the UB is ours and needs fixing
    regardless of compiler. The placement-new over `mu_` in `DGSolnImpl::Map`
    (`new (&mu_) VectorWrapper(...)`, then continued use of the name `mu_`) is
    the one piece of the mapping code that is not obviously well-defined, and is
    worth reading first even though the failing DOF is a trace rather than a
    scalar.

  Until then the build warns when it sees g++-14 (Makefile.config), and CI's two
  g++-14 legs should be treated as capable of failing for reasons unrelated to
  the change under review.

Investigate occasional wiggles in outputs.

Left over from the TransportSystem interface work. Everything else in that plan
landed; these are the pieces that were deliberately not done, with the reason.

  - Per-hook vectorisation in the Python trampoline. `override_for("SigmaFn_v")`
    and the other `_v` names are looked up but never inserted into
    method_overrides, so they can only throw: implementing `ComputePhysics` and
    `ComputePhysicsDerivatives` together remains the only vectorised route. A
    case that wants to vectorise one hook and leave the rest pointwise cannot.
    Registering the `_v` names in initializeOverrides is most of the fix; the
    rest is dropping the all-or-nothing classification.

  - The autodiff Source still receives the *stored* sigma, not sigma_hat.
    State::sigmaHat exists and is correct, but AutodiffTransportSystem::Sources
    builds its RealVector from the stored (negated) value, so a case that starts
    using its sigma argument gets the opposite sign from the one SigmaFn
    returned. Flipping it also needs a compensating negation in
    dSources_dsigma, because autodiff would then differentiate with respect to
    sigma_hat while the solver wants d/d(stored sigma).

    The reason this is not already done: *nothing in the tree reads the incoming
    sigma at all*. AdjointPlasma threads it into Sn/Spi/Spe without using it --
    as the C++ MirrorPlasma did before it was removed -- and every
    dSources_dsigma is identically
    zero. So the change is unobservable today and no test would catch getting it
    wrong -- which is exactly why it should not be made blind. Write a physics
    case with a genuinely sigma-dependent source first, check it against a
    closed form, then flip.

  - GlobalState still crosses to Python as a dict of (nPoints, nVars) arrays
    keyed "Variable"/"Derivative"/"Flux"/"Aux"/"Scalars". State became a named
    view; GlobalState did not, because a dict of whole-batch arrays is what the
    vectorised and JAX paths actually want. Worth revisiting only if the key
    strings start causing the same trouble the pointwise ones did.

  - The out-of-tree paths are verified by hand, not by CI. Both were exercised
    end to end -- a one-file C++ case compiled against the installed headers,
    dlopened via PhysicsPlugins and run; and `pip install .` into a fresh venv
    with a Python case run from the user's own directory through the `manta`
    command -- but neither is an automated test. The C++ one needs `make
    install` to a temp prefix and a compiler invocation; the Python one needs a
    venv. Both are slow enough to want their own CI job rather than a place in
    `make test`.


Found since, and left undone deliberately rather than overlooked. Each says what
would have to exist before the fix could be checked, which in every case is the
reason it was not made:

  - PyAdjointProblem::dAux disagrees with the Python dAux it calls. The
    trampoline (PyAdjointProblem.hpp:217) hands the Python method a raw
    GlobalState, but JAXAdjointProblem.dAux expects a converted jax State,
    because the only caller that matters -- ComputePhysicsDerivatives, which is
    what SystemSolver.cpp:1661 invokes -- is @Physics_Decorator and converts
    before calling it. So `AdjointProblem.dAux(...)` from Python raises a vmap
    in_axes error, while the path the solver takes works. dSigma and dSources
    have the same shape and presumably the same split.

    Harmless today: nothing in the tree calls the trampoline. Left alone
    because fixing it means choosing which contract the Python dAux has, and
    the decorated-state one is load-bearing for ComputePhysicsDerivatives --
    so the honest fix is probably to stop binding these three, the way
    ScalarGPrime and ComputePhysicsDerivatives already are not. Found while
    fixing the JAXAuxTest xfail; it cost a wrong turn there, because a test
    driving AdjointProblem.dAux looks like it exercises the solver's path and
    does not.

  - No C++ test asserts that a scalar declared differential has a time
    derivative in its G. Without one, IDACalcIC is handed a row whose every
    quantity it holds fixed, and it fails with IDA_LINESEARCH_FAIL (-13) before
    the first step -- a message about the linesearch, for a defect in the
    declaration. That is what kept python-physics/mirror-plasma's voltage
    controller from ever starting, and the C++ MirrorPlasma carried the same
    misdeclaration before it was removed, so this is not specific to the Python
    surface. The Python side now pins it in
    test_a_differential_scalars_constraint_reaches_a_calcic_unknown; the C++
    equivalent belongs next to checkScalarDerivative in ScalarJacobianTests.cpp
    and is cheap -- call ScalarGPrime and assert dGdot is not identically zero
    for each differential scalar. Two of the three fixtures there would have to
    be checked against it first.

  - The objective estimate has no in-loop consumer yet.

    estimateObjective() is callable at any point and leaves alpha and the
    factorised blocks as it found them, so a continuation step can ask it. What
    the loop cannot do is *act* on the answer: solveSteadyState has three ways
    out and two of them throw, so there is no path for "stop here, this is good
    enough" or "stop here, this cannot beat the incumbent". SteadyOutcome records
    which exit was taken and the estimate is filled before either throw, so a
    caught failure carries a usable partial answer, and MaxContinuationSteps plus
    continueSteadyState() turn that into a workable slice-and-inspect loop: a
    solve cut short resumes at the state and the pseudo-time step it reached, at
    no measured cost in continuation steps. That is enough for a caller willing
    to look between slices. A clean early return from *inside* a step still needs
    adding, along with whatever criterion would drive it.

    Before adding one, note what the measurements say about the criterion. An
    early exit keyed on the *bound* is worth much less than it sounds: the
    objective's error is proportional to ||J^-1 F||, and driving ||F|| down is the
    whole of the solve, so there is no tail to cut. Deciding G to 1% still costs
    95% of the solve and even a 50% decision costs 80%. The win is in solving
    looser and correcting -- 34% cheaper and three times more accurate than
    converging two orders harder and taking G raw -- which needs no early exit at
    all, only a caller willing to set SteadyStateTolerance and read `corrected`.

    So the in-loop hook is for a criterion nobody has yet: abandoning a parameter
    point against an incumbent the caller supplies. That is worth building when
    there is a sweep to measure it on.

    The slice loop is reachable from Python as start_steady / continue_steady /
    finish_steady, wrapped as manta.SteadySolve.

  - Degree adaptation and a sliced steady solve are refused together, and the
    combination that would be worth having is not the one that was refused.

    start_steady() throws when DegreeAdaptation is set. The mechanical reason is
    that adapting the degree *replaces* the SystemSolver, once per degree
    (PyRunner::adaptDegree), while a slice loop holds the state of the one it
    started on -- so the loop would be resuming into a solver that no longer
    exists. Refusing is right for now: it is one line, and it fails where the
    mistake was made rather than several slices later.

    What is actually wanted is the other order. Slice to a converged state at the
    degree you have, raise the degree, and carry on from the answer you already
    reached -- which is a *restart* rather than a continuation, because the
    coefficients move to a different space. setInitialConditions already does
    that transfer for a restart file, and knows the difference: a degree change
    is projected, so sigma is rebuilt and the state it hands the solver is a
    guess like any other. The trace survives verbatim, since lambda has no
    polynomial degree.

    So the pieces exist and the sequencing is the work: after a converged slice,
    project the state onto the new degree, rebuild the solver, and re-enter the
    continuation. Note the pseudo-time step should *not* transfer with it -- the
    projected state is off the manifold in a way the old dt was not chosen for,
    which is the same reason a projected restart runs IDACalcIC where a copied
    one does not.

  - Two things about the estimate are unmeasured and would change its sizing.

      * The norm. The bound pairs unweighted 2-norms. Pairing the WRMS norm with
        its dual should be tighter, and is the norm IDA's own error test would
        recognise.
      * Whether it transfers to a time-marching run. Only steady solves were
        measured, and there G is a property of the fixed point rather than of a
        path, which is what makes J^-1 F the right correction.

    The stellarator's cost profile is the other open question -- a solve that
    spends many steps at low residual would change the arithmetic above -- and
    needs a machine that can hold the real resolution: the full case wants ~88 GB
    for the drift-kinetic solve, and the reduced one does not converge.

Superconvergence follow-ons (see Tests/README.md for what is already verified):

  - Paper II's HDG(ABC) / HDG+ family: W_h = P_{k+1} with tau ~ 1/h makes u_h
    itself superconverge, with no postprocessing, and reaches k+2 at k = 0 too.
    Needs mixed polynomial degrees across fields, which the single-k assumption in
    DGSoln::Map, every block in Matrices.cpp, AdjointVectors.cpp, the pybind11
    casters and the restart format all rest on. tau ~ 1/h also changes every
    existing run.

  - No manufactured case has more than one aux variable, more than one scalar, or
    both at once. The order studies cover nAux = 1 and nScalars = 1 separately, so
    a block-indexing error that only appears at the second of either would not
    show up in them. SystemSolverMatrixTests.cpp covers the layout with two
    scalars and one aux, and ScalarJacobianTests.cpp with three scalars, but
    neither measures a rate.

  - Spatial adjoint parameters with Superconvergent = true currently throw. They
    index the parameter vector by node, so the star node set would redefine how
    many parameters there are.


The regression suite compares only `u`, never the `q`, `sigma` and `u_star` that
every run also writes. Closing it means regenerating the references and picking
tolerances for three fields a derivative rougher than `u`. Tests/README.md's
"Regression tests" section has the detail and the evidence that it is real slack.


From the origin/optimize-mode review, still unmerged and worth taking:
  - the branch's Objective2.py changes. Two of them: grad_solver_config gains
    "solveAdjoint" = True, and the primal path stops going through
    StellaratorFun and builds the case directly for G and the pressure. Small,
    but objective2.py also does `from stellarator2 import StellaratorTransport`
    hardwired, which is the reason scan_eq_ambipolar.py is not yet coherent:
    it constructs stellarator_multichannel directly *and* calls make_objective,
    which builds the single-channel case. Making the class injectable -- a
    keyword defaulting to the present one -- would fix that without touching the
    four consumers configured the old way, but it is a design change on code
    nothing here can run, so it wants a decision rather than a guess.
  - promoting stellarator_multichannel.py over stellarator2.py, if that is
    wanted. It is a rename plus the st_config of objective2.py,
    desc_optimize.py, scan_eq.py, desc_optimize_bfgs.py and the notebook:
    SourceCenter/SourceHeight/SourceWidth -> HeatSourceCenter/Height/Width.
    Mechanical, but unverifiable here, so it was not done on spec.
  - why the branch removed both `#pragma omp parallel for` from
    TransportSystem.hpp's batched defaults -- ask eatocco; may be a real finding
    about OpenMP against a Python trampoline

  The drivers, stellarator_state.py and the multi-channel rewrite itself are in
  python-physics/stellarator/ now; that README says which of them run against
  what. shard_inputs came with the rewrite.
